 The structure of this paper is as follows.  Our main cosmological results are summarized in #ref(<fig--main-results>, supplement: [Fig.]) and in #ref(<tab--main-results>, supplement: [Tab.]), with the power spectrum and bispectrum measurements shown in #ref(<fig--pk-bk-plot>, supplement: [Fig.]), and bias parameter constraints depicted in #ref(<fig-bias>, supplement: [Fig.]) We begin by describing the observational data in §#ref(<sec--data>, supplement: none), before discussing our power spectrum and bispectrum estimators in §#ref(<sec--estimators>, supplement: none) §#ref(<sec--model>, supplement: none) presents our perturbative model for the power spectrum and bispectrum (including extension to the real-space power spectrum, to allow for a larger $k$-range), as well as the likelihood used in our analyses. The methodology is tested in §#ref(<sec--consistency-tests>, supplement: none), before we present the main results of our analysis in §#ref(<sec--results>, supplement: none). We conclude with a summary and discuss future work in §#ref(<sec--summary>, supplement: none), with additional parameter constraints given in @appen--full-constraints
Our measurements and likelihoods are made publicly available online.#footnote[See #link("https://github.com/oliverphilcox/BOSS-Without-Windows")[GitHub.com/oliverphilcox/BOSS-Without-Windows] and #link("https://github.com/oliverphilcox/full\_shape\_likelihoods")[GitHub.com/oliverphilcox/full\_shape\_likelihoods].]

= Datasets
<sec--data>

 The primary dataset of this work is the twelfth data-release (DR12) of the Baryon Oscillation Spectroscopic Survey (BOSS) #cite(<2015ApJS..219...12A>), part of SDSS-III #cite(<2011AJ....142...72E>). In total, the survey contains over a million galaxies, observed in two disjoint regions of the sky; the Northern and Southern galactic caps (NGC and SGC). We divide the survey into two contiguous redshift slices, hereafter ‘z1’ and ‘z3’, encompassing $0.2 < z < 0.5$ and $0.5 < z < 0.75$ respectively (with effective redshifts 0.38 and 0.61), giving a total of four chunks. This is the decomposition used in previous power spectrum analyses #cite(<2017MNRAS.466.2242B>) #cite(<2020JCAP...05..042I>) #cite(<2020JCAP...05..005D>, supplement: [e.g.,]) though we note that it mixes galaxies from the CMASS and LOWZ samples, which will have implications for the effective bias parameters. In full, we use the publicly available ‘CMASSLOWZTOT’ galaxy samples,#footnote[Available at #link("https://data.sdss.org//sas/dr12/boss/lss")[data.sdss.org/sas/dr12/boss/lss]] filtered by redshift, and define the survey window function using the public #smallcaps[mangle] mask (required for the estimators of §#ref(<sec--estimators>, supplement: none)) as well as a set of random particles, with fifty times the galaxy density. The latter are additionally used to define the overdensity field. As in previous works, we use the following galaxy weights: $ w_"tot" = (w_"rf"+ w_"fc"- 1)w_"sys", $ <eq--boss-weights>
encoding redshift-failure, fiber-collision and systematic effects respectively #cite(<2017MNRAS.466.2242B>).#footnote[Note that optimality weights (such as the FKP weight) are naturally included in the estimators of §#ref(<sec--estimators>, supplement: none)]

 To construct a Gaussian likelihood for our dataset, we require a covariance matrix, which is here obtained using a (publicly available) suite of 2048 ‘MultiDark-Patchy’ mock catalogs (hereafter #smallcaps[Patchy]) #cite(<2016MNRAS.456.4156K>) #cite(<2016MNRAS.460.1173R>). Each is generated using an approximate gravity solver, and calibrated to an $N$-body simulation, providing an approximate form for the galaxy survey. These utilize a similar survey mask to the BOSS data and are generated using the cosmology $ \{Omega_(m) = 0.307115, sigma_(8) = 0.8288, h = 0.6777, sum m_(nu)= 0\}. $ A weight is associated with each particle: $ w_"tot" = w_"veto"w_"fc", $ <eq--patchy-weights>
involving a veto mask and a fiber collision term. We use a separate random catalog for the #smallcaps[Patchy] simulations.

 Due to their approximate nature, the #smallcaps[Patchy] mocks are not sufficiently accurate to test our analysis pipeline. Instead, we make use of the 84 #smallcaps[Nseries] mock catalogs#footnote[Available at #link("https://www.ub.edu/bispectrum/page11.html")[www.ub.edu/bispectrum/page11.html]] #cite(<2017MNRAS.470.2617A>); these are computed from full $N$-body simulations (though are not fully independent) using similar selection functions and halo occupation distribution to the BOSS data, as well as full treatment of fiber collisions. The #smallcaps[Nseries] mocks use the true cosmology $ \{Omega_(m) = 0.286, sigma_(8) = 0.82, n_(s) = 0.97, h = 0.7, sum m_(nu)= 0\}. $ Unlike the BOSS data, galaxies are not split into ‘z1’ and ‘z3’ chunks; rather, the simulations include only the CMASS NGC sample, spanning $0.43 < z < 0.7$ (with effective redshift $z_"eff"= 0.56$). This has a different geometry to the BOSS data, but due to its large cumulative volume, provides a sensitive test of our methodology, in particular that of systematic and geometric effects. The dataset contains completeness weights and has an associated random catalog. Since the #smallcaps[Nseries] data uses a different survey geometry, it requires a different covariance matrix; here this is generated using 2048 #smallcaps[Patchy] simulations with the #smallcaps[Nseries] window function (hereafter dubbed ‘#smallcaps[Patchy-Nseries]’). In total, we have four different random catalogs - BOSS, #smallcaps[Patchy], #smallcaps[Nseries] and #smallcaps[Patchy-Nseries] - each of which are separately treated using the estimators of §#ref(<sec--estimators>, supplement: none). In all cases, data are converted to Cartesian coordinates assuming the fiducial cosmology: $ \{Omega_(m,"fid") = 0.31, h_"fid" = 0.676\} $, which is used to calibrate the geometric distortion parameters (_i.e._/* \ */ Alcock-Paczynski parameters) of §#ref(<sec--model>, supplement: none).

 Finally, we include a prior on the physical baryon density of $omega_(b)= 0.02268 plus.minus 0.0038$, from Big Bang Nucleosynthesis (BBN) considerations #cite(<2015JCAP...07..011A>) #cite(<2018ApJ...855..102C>) #cite(<2019JCAP...10..029S>); this has a similar width to that from _Planck_ #cite(label("2020A&A...641A...6P")). Whilst we keep the tilt, $n_(s)$, free in our baseline analyses, we additionally consider a scenario in which it is fixed to the _Planck_ best-fit ($n_(s) = 0.9649$), to allow for comparison with other full-shape analyses.

= Estimators
<sec--estimators>

 Previous works have constrained cosmology from the power spectrum and bispectrum by measuring the statistics using simple FKP-like estimators #cite(<1994ApJ...426...23F>) #cite(<2015PhRvD..92h3532S>) #cite(<2015MNRAS.451..539G>) #cite(<2015MNRAS.453L..11B>) #cite(<2017MNRAS.466.2242B>, supplement: [e.g.]). Since the true galaxy distribution is modulated by a survey mask, the spectra are convolved with a non-trivial function of this mask, which must be replicated in the theoretical model. Whilst this can be simply implemented using FFTs for the power spectrum, it is more difficult for the bispectrum, since one must perform a six-dimensional convolution integral. This has led to previous studies excising regions of the spectrum most affected by the window, leading to a loss of information. Here, we adopt a different approach, instead measuring the unwindowed power spectrum and bispectrum directly from the observational data.

 To do this, we use the quadratic and cubic estimators discussed in #cite(<2021PhRvD.103j3504P>) and #cite(<2021arXiv210706287P>). These are derived by first writing down the large-scale likelihood for the galaxy survey and analytically optimizing for the statistics of interest, in a manner similar to that used in early CMB and LSS analyses #cite(<1997PhRvD..55.5895T>) #cite(<2002MNRAS.335..887T>) #cite(<1998ApJ...499..555T>) #cite(<2005astro.ph..3604H>, supplement: [e.g.]). Schematically, the estimator for the power spectrum in some bin $alpha$ (comprising a set of wavenumbers and multipole) can be written: $ hat(p)_(alpha)= sum_(beta)F_(alpha beta)^(- 1)[ hat(q)_(beta)- overline(q)_(beta) ], $
where $hat(q)_(alpha)$ is a quadratic piece, $overline(q)_(alpha)$ is a bias term, and $F_(alpha beta)$ is a Fisher matrix. These can be computed in terms of the pixelized data-vector, $arrow(d)$, the associated covariance matrix, $sans(C) equiv lr(chevron.l arrow(d) arrow(d)^(T) chevron.r)$, and the noise covariance, $sans(N)$, as $ hat(q)_(alpha)= 1/2[ sans(C)^(- 1)arrow(d) ]^(T) sans(C)_(,alpha)[ sans(C)^(- 1)arrow(d) ], wide overline(q)_(alpha)= 1/2op("Tr") [ sans(C)^(- 1)sans(C)_(,alpha)sans(C)^(- 1)sans(N) ], wide F_(alpha beta) = 1/2op("Tr") [ sans(C)^(- 1)sans(C)_(,alpha)sans(C)^(- 1)sans(C)_(,beta) ], $
where $sans(C)_(,alpha)$ represents the derivative of the pixel covariance with respect to the signal of interest, and we note that both $sans(C)$ and $sans(N)$ both depend on the survey mask. In certain limits, the first piece is proportional to the conventional FKP power spectrum, whilst the second removes the Poissonian noise contribution, and the third acts to deconvolve the window. A similar form can be obtained for the bispectrum components, $ \{b_(alpha)\} $ (where $alpha$ specifies a triplet of bins): $ hat(b)_(alpha)= sum_(beta)cal(F)^(- 1)_(alpha beta)hat(c)_(beta), $
where $hat(c)_(alpha)$ is a cubic estimator and $cal(F)$ is the associated Fisher matrix (which is close to tridiagonal). These can be written in terms of the three-point expectation of the data: $sans(B)^(i j k) equiv lr(chevron.l d^(i)d^(j)d^(k) chevron.r)$ (using latin indices to denote pixels and employing Einstein summation), and take the form $ hat(c)_(alpha)= 1/6sans(B)^(i j k)_(,alpha)[ sans(C)^(- 1)arrow(d) ]_(i)([ sans(C)^(- 1)arrow(d) ]_(j)[ sans(C)^(- 1)arrow(d) ]_(k)- 3 sans(C)_(j k)^(- 1)), wide cal(F)_(alpha beta) = 1/6sans(B)_(,alpha)^(i j k)sans(B)_(,beta)^(l m n)sans(C)^(- 1)_(i l)sans(C)^(- 1)_(j m)sans(C)^(- 1)_(k n). $
Notably, $hat(c)_(alpha)$ contains a piece _linear_ in the data; this is not found in conventional LSS estimators (though commonplace for the CMB #cite(<2011MNRAS.417....2S>)), and helps to reduce variance on large scales. All quantities appearing in the power spectrum and bispectrum estimators can be efficiently computed using Monte Carlo methods, and give minimum variance estimates of the spectra.

 Here, we use the specific forms of the estimators suggested in #cite(<2021arXiv210706287P>), approximating the pixel covariance by a diagonal (FKP-like) form with $P_"fkp"approx 10^(4)h^(- 3)upright("Mpc")^(3)$, though still fully accounting for survey geometry.#footnote[Due to a minor error in the original code, the FKP weight differs from this value at the $tilde 10 %$ level; the impact of this is negligible in practice.] Whilst this leads to a slight loss of optimality, this approximation greatly reduces computation time and does not induce bias. The estimators are implemented using a Fourier-space grid with Nyquist frequency of $k_"Nyq" = 0.45 h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$ ($0.3 h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$) for the power spectrum (bispectrum), measuring all modes up to $k = 0.41 h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$ ($0.16 h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$) with $Delta k = 0.005 h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$ ($0.01 h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$), and using the power spectrum monopole, quadrupole and hexadecapole. We caution that the first and last bins will be contaminated with integral-constraint and window-leakage effects respectively; these are not included in the analysis below. In total we use 100 Monte Carlo simulations to compute the Fisher matrices (which gives only a subpercent noise penalty, cf. #cite(<2021arXiv210706287P>)), requiring a total of $approx 10 thin 000$ ($approx 3000$) CPU-hours for the power spectrum (bispectrum) of all four BOSS data chunks. Importantly, this only needs to be done once per survey geometry. The increased computation time for the power spectrum is due to both the larger number of bins (the computation time scales as $N_"bins"$) and the higher-resolution grid. The data-dependent part of the power spectrum (bispectrum) estimator requires $approx 15$ ($approx 10$) CPU-minutes to compute per simulation, and thus can be easily applied to all $2048$ #smallcaps[Patchy] mocks.

 Using the output of the above estimators, we form the following datasets, which will be analyzed in §#ref(<sec--results>, supplement: none) (cf. #ref(<fig--main-results>, supplement: [Fig.])):
- *Redshift-Space Power Spectrum, $P_(ell)(k)$*: Here, we use the power spectrum monopole, quadrupole and hexadecapole (with $ell in \{0,2,4\}$) with $k in [0.01,0.20 ]h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$ and $Delta k = 0.005 h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$. Our $k$-space cuts are motivated by the results of #cite(<2020PhRvD.102l3541N>) #cite(<2021PhRvD.103b3507C>). These are corrected for the effects of survey geometry and given by the outputs of the above estimators. We use a total of $38$ bins for each multipole.
- *Real-Space Power Spectrum, $Q_(0)(k)$*: Following #cite(<2021arXiv211000006I>) (see also the earlier works of #cite(<Hamilton:2000du>) #cite(<Scoccimarro:2004tg>)), we use an analog to the real-space power spectrum computed from the above redshift-space power spectrum multipoles up to $ell = 4$, explicitly via $Q_(0)(k) equiv P_(0)(k)- 1/2P_(2)(k)+ 3/8P_(4)(k)$. This is included for $k in [0.2,0.4 ]h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$ (involving $40$ bins) and allows cosmological information to be included without the limitations of fingers-of-God modeling.
- *BAO Parameters, $alpha_(parallel), alpha_(perp)$*: To capture sound-horizon information present in the reconstructed power spectrum, we include the parallel and perpendicular BAO scaling parameters, as discussed in #cite(<2020JCAP...05..032P>). These are measured for all data chunks in the BOSS and #smallcaps[Patchy] datasets, and correlations are included via a joint covariance matrix (see #cite(<2021arXiv211005530C>) for an alternative approach). We note that only 999 mocks are available for these parameters.
- *Bispectrum, $B_(0)(k_(1),k_(2),k_(3))$*: The bispectrum monopole is obtained from the above cubic estimators.#footnote[Strictly, the estimate of the bispectrum monopole is unbiased only if one measures all possible bispectrum multipoles, due to coupling of the redshift-space contributions with the anisotropic window function (analogous to the power spectrum case). Here, we assume this effect to be small, though its magnitude will be studied in future work.] To avoid window leakage and poorly modelled regimes, we use only $k$-modes in the range $[0.01,0.08 ]h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$ and $Delta k = 0.01 h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$, whose centers satisfy the triangle condition $| k_(1)- k_(2)| <= k_(3)<= k_(1)+ k_(2)$ #cite(<2021arXiv211010161I>).#footnote[Note that triangles violating this condition must be included in the initial bispectrum estimator, to avoid bias, though they can be dropped when the statistic is analyzed.] In total, we include $62$ bispectrum bins.
 Each statistic is computed for the BOSS data, the #smallcaps[Patchy] mocks, the #smallcaps[Nseries] mocks and the #smallcaps[Patchy-Nseries] mocks, and the data, estimation pipeline, and likelihoods are made publicly available online.#footnote[See #link("https://github.com/oliverphilcox/BOSS-Without-Windows")[GitHub.com/oliverphilcox/BOSS-Without-Windows] and #link("https://github.com/oliverphilcox/full\_shape\_likelihoods")[GitHub.com/oliverphilcox/full\_shape\_likelihoods].] The total data-vector contains $N_"bin"= 114 + 40 + 2 + 62 = 218$ elements, and is shown in #ref(<fig--pk-bk-plot>, supplement: [Fig.]) (cf. §#ref(<sec--results>, supplement: none)).

