== Baseline Analysis
<subsec--baseline>

=== Results
 We begin by analyzing the power spectrum, BAO parameters, and bispectrum in the manner described above, with all cosmological parameters (except the neutrino mass) free. The main results of this are displayed in #ref(<fig--boss-free-ns>, supplement: [Fig.]) (and summarized in #ref(<fig--main-results>, supplement: [Fig.])), with the associated parameter confidence intervals given in #ref(<tab--free-ns>, supplement: [Tab.]), with additional results (including best-fits, 95% confidence levels, and full corner plots including bias parameters) given in @appen--full-constraints. As more datasets are included in the analysis, we find that the mean parameter values are generally consistent, but the error-bars reduce.

 The addition of $Q_(0)$ gives a slight improvement in the precision with which a variety of parameters can be measured; this arises due to the inclusion of smaller-scale power spectrum information, the importance of which is limited by the high BOSS shot-noise #cite(<2021arXiv211000006I>). When BAO parameters are included, the $H_(0)$ contour shrinks by approximately $20 %$, matching the conclusion of #cite(<2020JCAP...05..032P>). Here, the extra constraining power is sourced by higher-order statistics, whose sound-horizon information is transferred to the power spectrum, following BAO reconstruction #cite(<2015PhRvD..92l3522S>). Finally, when we add the bispectrum monopole, the $sigma_(8)$ posterior shrinks by $13 %$ (in accordance with the $N$-body results of #cite(<2021arXiv211010161I>)), though other projections remain consistent. In this case, information arises from broadband features in the bispectrum, and allows for degeneracy breaking, which acts to tight the constraint on the fluctuation amplitude. Notably, the $H_(0)$ posterior is not tightened - this occurs since the large-scale bispectrum does not include BAO signatures (since we truncate at $k = 0.08 h thin upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$). Information present on smaller scales is already included via the BAO parameters (see #cite(<2018MNRAS.478.4500P>) for a detection of this from the three-point function alone). Although the bispectrum is not found to give particularly large improvements in the error-bars of cosmological parameters, this is primarily due to our conservative choices of $k_"max"$ and bias parameter priors, and would change if one accepted a larger systematic error budget or developed the theoretical modeling further #cite(<2021arXiv211010161I>). If one is instead interested in galaxy formation physics, the bispectrum's addition improves constraints significantly; this is discussed in §#ref(<subsec--bias-results>, supplement: none)

=== Cosmological Interpretation
<subsec--comparison> The parameter contours found above can be straightforwardly compared to those of other analyses, both making use of LSS data, and other sources. For the Hubble constant, we find a marginalized constraint of $H_(0) = 69.6^(+ 1.1)_(- 1.3)thin upright("km") thin upright(s)^(- 1)upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$ when all datasets are combined. This is consistent with that reported in #cite(<2021arXiv211010161I>) (using $P_(ell)$ and $Q_(0)$ information), but somewhat higher than the results of #cite(<2020JCAP...05..042I>) #cite(<2020JCAP...05..032P>). The difference is thought to arise from the power spectrum measurements themselves: in this work, and #cite(<2021arXiv211010161I>), we do not use public data products, but instead remeasure the spectra from scratch (cf. §#ref(<subsec--pk-analysis-comparison>, supplement: none)). Here, this is achieved via window-free estimators, which §#ref(<sec--consistency-tests>, supplement: none) show to give robust parameter constraints. Our $H_(0)$ posterior is somewhat lower than those obtained from the Cepheid-calibrated distance ladder #cite(<2021ApJ...908L...6R>): $H_(0)^"SH0ES" = 73.2 plus.minus 1.3 thin upright("km") thin upright(s)^(- 1)upright("Mpc")^(- 1)$, though, given the large error-bars, it is difficult to draw any firm conclusions. The $Omega_(m)$ posterior is consistent with that of the Pantheon sample: $Omega_(m)^"Pantheon" = 0.298 plus.minus 0.022$ #cite(<2018ApJ...859..101S>), and the $n_(s)$ posterior is broad, but in agreement with that from _Planck_: $n_(s)^"Planck" = 0.9649 plus.minus 0.0042$.

 The $sigma_(8)$ results are of particular interest. Our full analysis finds $sigma_(8) = 0.69 plus.minus 0.04$ (or $sigma_(8)= 0.70 plus.minus 0.05$, using the power spectrum multipoles alone), which is somewhat different from that of previous full-shape power spectrum analyses #cite(<2020JCAP...05..042I>) #cite(<2021PhRvD.103b3538P>) #cite(<2020JCAP...05..005D>) #cite(<2021arXiv211006969K>). Comparison with the old analyses in not straightforward, since they differ in many aspects, such as the inclusion of the hexadecapole moment in the data and window function model, the choice of sampling parameters, e.g. whether to use $A_(s) slash A_(s,"Planck")$ or $ln (10^(10)A_(s))$, and finally, the priors on nuisance parameters. If we analyze the power spectrum data used by the former works with the same theory model and data cuts as our baseline analysis (see §#ref(<subsec--pk-analysis-comparison>, supplement: none)), and consistently treat the window function, we find $sigma_(8)= 0.66 plus.minus 0.05$, which is significantly lower than the _Planck_ value, and $1 sigma$ below our current bound.

 This difference is entirely caused by an error in the public BOSS power spectra,#footnote[Available at #link("https://fbeutler.github.io/hub/boss_papers.html")[fbeutler.GitHub.io/hub/boss\_papers.html]] whereupon the spectra were incorrectly suppressed by a constant factor $approx 10 %$. This occurred due to an invalid approximation in the power spectrum normalization: the geometric factor $A equiv integral d bold(r) thin overline(n)^(2)(bold(r))w_"fkp"^(2)(bold(r))$ appearing in the standard FKP power spectrum estimator was replaced by a sum over random particles: $ A_"approx" = frac(N_(upright(g)), N_(upright(r)))sum_(i thin in thin "randoms") overline(n)_(g)(z_(i))w^(2)_"fkp,i"thin, $
for approximate redshift dependence $overline(n)_(g)(z_(i))$, using a (weighted) total of $N_"gal"$ galaxies and $N_"rand"$ randoms #cite(<2017MNRAS.464.3409B>) #cite(<2017MNRAS.466.2242B>) #cite(<2017MNRAS.465.1757G>) #cite(<2021arXiv211006969K>). This approximation is valid only at the $approx 10 %$ level for the BOSS sample, and the power spectra should strictly be normalized by the $r -> 0$ limit of the window function $W(r)$ (which is equal to the two-point correlation function of the random particles) #cite(<2021JCAP...11..031B>) #cite(<2021MNRAS.501.5616D>).#footnote[We caution that this normalization is still used in the #smallcaps[nbodykit] code #cite(<2018AJ....156..160H>), and was also present in the reanalysis of #cite(<2021arXiv211000006I>). To correct for it, one must similarly normalize the window function by $A_"approx"$, instead of the usual approach, which sets $lim_(r -> 0)W(r) = 1$.] At linear order, $P(k,mu) prop [ b + f mu^(2) ]^(2)sigma^(2)_(8)$, thus this has the effect of reducing the value of $sigma_(8)$ by $tilde 5 %$ relative to its true value. This resulted in a a series of works that found $sigma_(8)$ to be in (erroneous) mild tension with _Planck_. In our approach, we do not require the 2PCF of the mask to be computed explicitly, thus are not affected by such problems. Our results for $sigma_(8)$ are consistent with those found in #cite(<2021arXiv211005530C>), which uses the correctly normalized spectra.

 Recent works have found a growing tendency for weak-lensing analyses to predict lower clustering amplitudes than that of _Planck_; an anomaly that shows greater consistency between observables than the much-ballyhooed ‘$H_(0)$ tension’. In terms of the $S_(8) equiv sigma_(8)(Omega_(m) slash 0.3)^(0.5)$ parameter, _Planck_ finds $S_(8)^(P l a n c k) = 0.832 plus.minus 0.012$ #cite(label("2020A&A...641A...6P")), whilst the latest weak lensing results from the latest Dark Energy Survey $3 times 2$ analysis finds $S_(8)^"DES" = 0.776 plus.minus 0.017$ #cite(<2021arXiv210513549D>). An additional constraint is obtained from cross-correlating galaxy surveys with the CMB: this obtained $S_(8) = 0.776 plus.minus 0.017$ for the unWISE sample #cite(<2021arXiv210503421K>), and $S_(8) = 0.73 plus.minus 0.03$ when using the DESI imaging survey #cite(<2021arXiv211109898W>). Here, we find $S_(8) = 0.734^(+ 0.035)_(- 0.041)$; this is consistent with the other low-redshift observations, but differs from _Planck_ at the $2.5 sigma$ level. The upcoming tranche of spectroscopic and photometric survey data will be able to shed light on whether this is indeed a _bona fide_ discrepancy (the prospect of which is discussed in §#ref(<subsec--s8-physics>, supplement: none)), or simply a statistical fluctuation.

